{{ElectionsCO}}
The '''1994 Colorado gubernatorial election''' was held on November 8, 1994, to select the governor of the state of [[Colorado]]. Although Colorado voters passed a term limits ballot measure in 1990 limiting the governors to two consecutive terms, it was not retroactive and only applied for terms beginning in 1991 or later, thus [[Roy Romer]], the Democratic incumbent first elected in 1986, was able to run for reelection for a third term.<ref>{{Cite web |title=1990BlueBook.pdf {{!}} State of Colorado |url=https://historicalelectiondata.coloradosos.gov/document/196?page=21 |access-date=2025-07-27 |website=State of Colorado Elections Database |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Colorado Amendment No. 5, Term Limits for Certain Offices Initiative (1990) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Colorado_Amendment_No._5,_Term_Limits_for_Certain_Offices_Initiative_(1990) |access-date=2025-07-27 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url = https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1994/09/29/chill-wind-blowing-for-democrats-in-rocky-mountain-states/20c123e6-a146-4ab5-a477-77ca0e39de3c/| title = CHILL WIND BLOWING FOR DEMOCRATS IN ROCKY MOUNTAIN STATES - The Washington Post| newspaper = [[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> The Republican nominee, Chairman of the [[Colorado Republican Party]], [[Bruce D. Benson]], lost by a margin of nearly 18 percent.

This election coincided with the [[Republican Revolution|Republican Revolution of 1994]].

